workedExamples.solution_text:
Substitute each value of $x$. For $x=-2$:
$\begin{aligned}y&=-\frac14(-2)^2+4\\y&=3\end{aligned}$

| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| $y$ | $0$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |

Plot each column as a point and join the points with a smooth curve. The negative coefficient makes the parabola open downward.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,<->},xmin=-5,xmax=5,ymin=-2,ymax=5,xtick={-4,-2,0,2,4},ytick={-1,1,2,3,4},grid=major,grid style={gridGrey},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-4.7:4.7,samples=100]{-0.25*x*x+4};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.5pt] coordinates {(-4,0) (-2,3) (0,4) (2,3) (4,0)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Calculate the power before applying an outside negative sign. For example, $6^{-2}=\frac1{36}$, while $-6^2=-36$.

| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| $6^x$ | $\frac1{36}$ | $\frac16$ | $1$ | $6$ | $36$ |
| $6^{-x}$ | $36$ | $6$ | $1$ | $\frac16$ | $\frac1{36}$ |
| $-6^x$ | $-\frac1{36}$ | $-\frac16$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-36$ |

For $y=6^x$, multiply by $6$ at each step. For $y=6^{-x}$, divide by $6$. For $y=-6^x$, multiply by $6$, giving increasingly negative values.

Plot the columns and draw smooth curves. All three approach $y=0$ without reaching it.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{growth}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{decay}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{reflection}{HTML}{4F9B63}
\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,<->},xmin=-2.5,xmax=2.5,ymin=-40,ymax=40,xtick={-2,-1,0,1,2},ytick={-36,-18,18,36},grid=major,grid style={gridGrey},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},legend style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,at={(0.5,1.03)},anchor=south,legend columns=1}]
\addplot[growth,thick,<->,domain=-2.4:2.05,samples=100]{exp(x*ln(6))};\addlegendentry{$y=6^x$}
\addplot[decay,thick,dashed,<->,domain=-2.05:2.4,samples=100]{exp(-x*ln(6))};\addlegendentry{$y=6^{-x}$}
\addplot[reflection,thick,dashdotted,<->,domain=-2.4:2.05,samples=100]{-exp(x*ln(6))};\addlegendentry{$y=-6^x$}
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.3pt,forget plot] coordinates {(-2,0.027777778) (-1,0.166666667) (0,1) (1,6) (2,36) (-2,36) (-1,6) (1,0.166666667) (2,0.027777778) (-2,-0.027777778) (-1,-0.166666667) (0,-1) (1,-6) (2,-36)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Substitute the times into the model. For $t=3$, $N=2(2^3)=16$ thousand.

| $t$ (hours) | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| $N$ (thousands) | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $16$ | $32$ |

Plot the columns and join them with a smooth exponential growth curve. The initial population is $2$ thousand and the common ratio is $2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=left,xmin=0,xmax=4.5,ymin=0,ymax=36,xtick={0,1,2,3,4},ytick={0,8,16,24,32},grid=major,grid style={gridGrey},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={Time $t$ (hours)},ylabel={Population $N$ (thousands)}]
\addplot[curveBlue,thick,domain=0:4,samples=100]{2*exp(x*ln(2))};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.5pt] coordinates {(0,2) (1,4) (2,8) (3,16) (4,32)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $t=3$:
$\begin{aligned}h&=80-5(3)^2\\h&=35\end{aligned}$

| $t$ (seconds) | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| $h$ (metres) | $80$ | $75$ | $60$ | $35$ | $0$ |

Plot each column and draw a smooth curve. Use $0\le t\le4$: the model ends when the object reaches the ground.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=left,xmin=0,xmax=4.5,ymin=0,ymax=90,xtick={0,1,2,3,4},ytick={0,20,40,60,80},grid=major,grid style={gridGrey},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={Time $t$ (seconds)},ylabel={Height $h$ (metres)}]
\addplot[curveBlue,thick,domain=0:4,samples=100]{80-5*x*x};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.5pt] coordinates {(0,80) (1,75) (2,60) (3,35) (4,0)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Substitute each value into both equations.

| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| $x^2+1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ |
| $3^x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ | $27$ |

Plot both sets of points and draw separate smooth curves. Both pass through $(0,1)$; the exponential rises more rapidly over this interval.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{quadratic}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{exponential}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=left,xmin=0,xmax=3.4,ymin=0,ymax=30,xtick={0,1,2,3},ytick={0,5,10,15,20,25,30},grid=major,grid style={gridGrey},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},legend style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,at={(0.02,0.98)},anchor=north west}]
\addplot[quadratic,thick,domain=0:3,samples=100]{x*x+1};\addlegendentry{$y=x^2+1$}
\addplot[exponential,thick,dashed,domain=0:3,samples=100]{exp(x*ln(3))};\addlegendentry{$y=3^x$}
\addplot[quadratic,only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.5pt,forget plot] coordinates {(0,1) (1,2) (2,5) (3,10)};
\addplot[exponential,only marks,mark=square*,mark size=1.5pt,forget plot] coordinates {(0,1) (1,3) (2,9) (3,27)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

